      </nav>

      <article>
        <p class="lead">You are probably less of an AI expert than you feel, and so is nearly everyone else. When a tool answers instantly and fluently, that ease gets mistaken for understanding. <strong>Real AI literacy is not how comfortable you are with ChatGPT. It is whether you know how it works, when to trust it, and when it is quietly wrong.</strong></p>

        <p>The evidence is uncomfortable. When researchers at Aalto University asked around 500 people to solve reasoning problems with ChatGPT, almost everyone overestimated how well they had done, and the people who rated themselves as most AI-literate were the most overconfident of all (<a href="https://www.aalto.fi/en/news/ai-use-makes-us-overestimate-our-cognitive-performance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aalto University, 2025</a>). In a separate 2026 survey of 1,500 workers, 63% admitted exaggerating their AI skills to look more capable, rising to 80% among Gen Z, while only 34% could confidently perform every AI skill they claimed (<a href="https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/05/19/3297597/0/en/63-of-Workers-Admit-to-Exaggerating-AI-Skills-as-Automation-Anxiety-Fuels-an-AI-Skills-Bubble-New-GCheck-Report-Finds.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GCheck, 2026</a>). Most people sense they are bluffing. Few have ever checked.</p>

        <div class="cav">Be honest for a second</div>
        <h2 id="overrate">Why do most people overrate their AI knowledge?</h2>
        <p>The short answer is that AI removes the friction that normally tells you when you are struggling. Type a question, get a polished paragraph back, and your brain reads the smooth result as proof you understood the problem. In the Aalto experiments, most people prompted ChatGPT only once per question rather than probing or checking it, so they never hit the moment of feedback that exposes a shaky grasp. The classic pattern where novices know the least about their own limits did not just hold, it reversed: the more AI-literate people believed they were, the more they overestimated.</p>
        <p>Self-rating, in other words, is close to useless as a measure of skill. A study presented at the 2026 Learning Analytics and Knowledge conference compared what people said about their AI literacy with how they scored on an objective test and found only a weak link between the two, with a clear group who rated themselves high and performed lower (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zhang et al., LAK26, 2026</a>). The takeaway is simple. If you want to know how good you are at AI, feeling confident is not the answer. Being tested is.</p>

        <div class="cav">More than prompting</div>
        <h2 id="literacy">What does AI literacy actually mean?</h2>
        <p>AI literacy is broader than knowing how to prompt a chatbot. UNESCO defines it across four dimensions: a human-centered mindset, the ethics of AI, AI techniques and applications, and AI system design, each progressing from understanding to applying to creating (<a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-students" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNESCO, 2024</a>). Put plainly, being AI-literate means you can use AI well, judge what it gives you, and understand enough about how it works to know where it fails. The Best Answer Hub AI Knowledge Test measures this across five practical domains.</p>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Foundations and concepts.</b> What AI, machine learning, and large language models are, and the words that actually describe them.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Everyday use and applications.</b> Prompting, picking the right tool, and getting reliable results on real tasks.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>How generative AI works.</b> Tokens, context windows, training cutoffs, and why the model behaves the way it does.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Critical evaluation and limitations.</b> Spotting hallucinations, checking claims, and knowing when a confident answer is wrong.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Ethics, bias, and responsible use.</b> What data is unsafe to share, where bias creeps in, and keeping a human in the loop.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">Where most people are strong, and weak</div><p>Comfort with everyday use is common. The gaps almost always sit in domains three and four: understanding how the model really works, and evaluating whether to believe it. Those are the domains that separate a confident user from a capable one.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The uncomfortable bit</div>
        <h2 id="gap">Why doesn’t confidence equal competence?</h2>
        <p>Because nothing forces the two to line up. You can use AI every day, feel completely at home with it, and still hold a flawed mental model and weak verification habits. The 2026 GCheck survey put numbers on the bluff: 63% of workers admit exaggerating their AI skills, only 34% can confidently do everything they claim, and 64% say their employer has never once checked (<a href="https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/05/19/3297597/0/en/63-of-Workers-Admit-to-Exaggerating-AI-Skills-as-Automation-Anxiety-Fuels-an-AI-Skills-Bubble-New-GCheck-Report-Finds.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GCheck, 2026</a>). The gap between claimed and real skill is wide, and mostly invisible.</p>

        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">The gap between claimed and real AI skill</figcaption>
