          <p class="chart-src">Source: GCheck Automation Anxiety Report, 2026 (survey of 1,500 US workers). 63% admit exaggerating their AI skills; 34% can confidently perform every skill they claim.</p>
        </figure>

        <p>There is a second trap hiding inside everyday use: over-trust. Large language models are trained to sound helpful and agreeable, so a wrong answer often arrives wrapped in a confident tone. Research on this sycophancy effect shows people rate self-assured, agreeable answers as higher quality and trust them more, even when they are less accurate. It shows up in real work, too. A 2026 Workday study of 3,200 employees found that nearly 40% of the time AI saves is then spent correcting, verifying, or rewriting its output (<a href="https://newsroom.workday.com/2026-01-14-New-Workday-Research-Companies-Are-Leaving-AI-Gains-on-the-Table" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Workday, 2026</a>). The skill that protects you is not faster prompting. It is knowing when and how to check.</p>

        <blockquote>Confidence is a poor guide to competence. <span class="accent">The people most certain they understand AI are often the ones who overestimate most.</span></blockquote>

        <div class="cav">If you remember one thing</div>
        <h2 id="model">The one idea that separates beginners from experts</h2>
        <p>If there is a single concept that marks the line between feeling fluent and being fluent, it is this: a large language model is a prediction engine, not a fact database. Many people quietly picture ChatGPT as a search engine with a friendly voice, looking up stored answers. It does not work that way. As OpenAI explains, its models do not keep copies of the data they were trained on; they adjust internal patterns during training and then predict the most likely next word, one word at a time (<a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7842364-how-chatgpt-and-our-foundation-models-are-developed" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI, 2026</a>).</p>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">Why this one idea changes everything</div><p>Once you see AI as predicting plausible text rather than retrieving verified facts, its quirks stop being surprising. Hallucinations, confident wrong answers, and made-up sources are not glitches in a database. They are the expected behavior of a system built to sound right, which is exactly why your judgment, not the model’s confidence, has to be the final check.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Why it is worth it</div>
        <h2 id="matters">Why does AI literacy matter now?</h2>
        <p>Because it has moved from a nice-to-have to something measured in pay and hiring. PwC analysis of close to a billion job ads found that roles requiring AI skills carried a 56% wage premium, more than double the year before (<a href="https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/news-room/press-releases/2025/ai-linked-to-a-fourfold-increase-in-productivity-growth.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PwC, 2025</a>). Analysing 1.3 billion postings, Lightcast put the premium at 28% higher salaries, roughly $18,000 more a year, and found that more than half of the jobs asking for AI skills now sit outside IT (<a href="https://lightcast.io/resources/blog/beyond-the-buzz-press-release-2025-07-23" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lightcast, 2025</a>).</p>

        <figure class="chart">
