            <line x1="70" y1="232" x2="560" y2="232" stroke="#E1D4be" stroke-width="2"/>
            <rect x="150" y="82" width="120" height="150" rx="8" fill="url(#paygrad)"/>
            <text x="210" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="27" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">56%</text>
            <text x="210" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">Wage premium (PwC)</text>
            <rect x="350" y="157" width="120" height="75" rx="8" fill="url(#paygrad)" opacity="0.78"/>
            <text x="410" y="147" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="27" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">28%</text>
            <text x="410" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">Salary premium (Lightcast)</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">Sources: PwC 2025 Global AI Jobs Barometer (56% wage premium); Lightcast, 2025 (28% salary premium, about $18,000 a year).</p>
        </figure>

        <p>The bar to get hired is rising at the same time. Microsoft and LinkedIn found that 66% of leaders would not hire someone without AI skills, yet only 39% of people had received any AI training at work (<a href="https://news.microsoft.com/source/2024/05/08/microsoft-and-linkedin-release-the-2024-work-trend-index-on-the-state-of-ai-at-work/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft and LinkedIn, 2024</a>). The World Economic Forum expects 39% of workers’ core skills to be disrupted by 2030 (<a href="https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-future-of-jobs-report-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WEF, 2025</a>). No wonder the anxiety is widespread: 90% of jobseekers say they are concerned about AI at work (<a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-is-driving-workplace-gains-but-deepening-job-anxiety-for-us-workers-302794236.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Express and Harris Poll, 2026</a>).</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">A note on the EU AI Act</div><p>Since 2 February 2025, Article 4 of the EU AI Act has required organizations that use AI to ensure their staff have a sufficient level of AI literacy, and the rule covers everyday tools like ChatGPT, not just high-risk systems (<a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 4</a>). Importantly, the law deliberately mandates no exam, score, or certificate. So treat this test as an honest mirror of where you stand, never as proof of compliance or a credential.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Find your starting line</div>
        <h2 id="levels">Beginner, Intermediate, or Advanced: which are you?</h2>
        <p>Real AI knowledge tends to fall into three broad levels. The level is not a grade. It is a starting point that tells you what to focus on next.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Score</th><th>What it usually looks like</th><th>Focus next on</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Beginner</td><td><span class="score lv1">0–49</span></td><td>Uses AI for simple tasks, tends to trust the output</td><td>The basics: what AI is, safe use, checking answers</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Intermediate</td><td><span class="score lv2">50–79</span></td><td>Comfortable daily, shaky on how it works and its limits</td><td>Mental model, structured prompts, verification habits</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Advanced</td><td><span class="score lv3">80–100</span></td><td>Understands the model, prompts and verifies deliberately</td><td>Edge cases, ethics, evaluating and stress-testing output</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The catch is that guessing your own level is exactly the thing people get wrong, as the overestimation research shows. Most people assume they are Intermediate or Advanced and are surprised by where an objective test actually places them, usually because of domains three and four.</p>

        <div class="cav">Your turn</div>
        <h2 id="find">How do you find out where you really stand?</h2>
        <p>You take a test that checks what you can do, not how you feel. The free AI Knowledge Test serves 50 questions across the five domains, scores each one objectively, and shows your level plus a radar breakdown of your strongest and weakest areas, so you learn where the real gaps are rather than where you assume they are.</p>
        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">See where you really stand</span>
          <h3>Take the free AI Knowledge Test</h3>
          <p>50 questions, about 20 minutes, no signup and no email. Your score, level, and domain radar are computed in your browser and never uploaded.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/knowledge-center/ai-knowledge-test/">Start the free test</a>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">One step at a time</div>
        <h2 id="improve">How do you actually get better at AI?</h2>
        <p>Not by chasing every new tool. The fastest route is to pick one tool and one recurring task, then build a habit. Microsoft research found that the people who gain the most from AI are the ones who experiment consistently and reorganize one workflow at a time, saving more than 30 minutes a day (<a href="https://news.microsoft.com/source/2024/05/08/microsoft-and-linkedin-release-the-2024-work-trend-index-on-the-state-of-ai-at-work/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft and LinkedIn, 2024</a>). Depth on a single use beats shallow familiarity with ten.</p>
        <p>There is a quieter payoff, too. Building real understanding is what calms the anxiety. A peer-reviewed study found that higher AI literacy was associated with lower job-seeking anxiety, because understanding AI, rather than avoiding it, is what restores a sense of control (<a href="https://bmcpsychology.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s40359-025-02757-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BMC Psychology, 2025</a>). Knowing where you stand is the first step, and it costs nothing but 20 minutes.</p>

